  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 \nHigh level cirrus clouds will continue to stream over the area \nthis morning, gradually becoming a bit thicker and solid by late\nafternoon and evening. Otherwise, there could be some patchy \nvalley fog around this morning. Light southeast winds are \nforecast through the day, less than 10 mph. Overall, it will be \npartly sunny with filter sunshine through the high clouds. It \nwill be seasonably warm with highs in the 60s.\n\nTonight will feature increasing, thickening and lowering cirrus \nclouds. It will be cool, especially east and north of Binghamton, \nwith lows in the 40s for most locations.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2\nThere still remains some minor uncertainty on the exact timing \nof the incoming rain Sunday morning. Some of the CAMs guidance \nis slower by 3-4 hours, and some quicker. Overall, it appears \nthe steady rain will overspread the areas fairly quickly through\nthe morning hours on Sunday...but potentially not reaching \nSyracuse, Utica, Rome and Cooperstown until Sunday afternoon. \nPeriods of steady rain continue through the day and evening \nhours, with also a slight chance for a thunderstorm. Otherwise, \nit will be overcast and cooler with highs in the mid-50s to \nlower 60s. East-southeast winds will increase, especially over \nthe ridgetops...between 8-15 mph with gusts up to 30 mph for the\nhilltops in NE PA. Total rainfall amounts have nudged back up \nto between 0.6 to 1.00 inches areawide. Current guidance has the\nrain quickly tapering off and ending after midnight Sunday \nnight.\n\nMonday is looking dry, with gradually decreasing clouds as an upper \nlevel ridge pushes in from the west. Winds shift northwesterly and \nmild high temperatures should reach well into the 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 \nA surface low is forecast to track to our north \nthrough the day on Tuesday. This will put our CWA in the warm \nsector, with southwest winds and warm temperatures expected. \nThere will also be some surface based instability as LIs drop to\n-2 and SBCAPE could reach 200-500 J/Kg...depending on boundary \nlayer destabilization and heating. If current timing hold, the \ncold front would approach the area late in the day or evening on\nTuesday. With the 00z GFS showing 30-40 kts of 0-6km bulk \nshear, we may need to watch for some organizing convection along\nor ahead of the front. Ensemble guidance is in generally good \nagreement with this frontal system, and chances for rain are \nincreasing (60-80% over the area). If we do get some breaks of \nsun, it could be quite warm on Tuesday with highs in the mid-60s\nto low 70s.\n\nBehind this frontal system the latest guidance keeps it mainly dry \nand seasonably cool for Wednesday and Thursday next week. The next \nchance for rain will be Friday, into Saturday as a potentially deep \ntrough approaches the forecast area. This could bring much cooler \ntemperatures for next weekend, stay tuned.",
